{-
   What an `alias` refuses.

   # resolver: ownership

   The cycle is the one a transparent alias can actually make: it stands for what it names, so a
   pair that name each other stands for nothing. Reported where the walk sees it rather than by
   running out of stack, which is the same three-colour walk `Reject.Rank.yana` uses for defaults.

   The `qualified` form is refused by name, and it is a declaration this stage does not have rather
   than a case it dropped: a qualified alias is a *type*, with conversions to and from its target and
   with `deriving` attaching instances to it. A `deriving` clause on a plain alias needs no refusal
   here at all - the parser admits one only on the qualified form, because a plain alias names the
   same type as its target and that type already has whatever instances it has.
-}

alias Loop = Ring
alias Ring = Loop

alias qualified Flags = I32

fn main() -> I32 = 0
